  "narrative": "All estimates and intervals are already signed to each claim's registered direction, so positive signed evidence supports the claim and negative signed evidence opposes it. The newly available response-deadline validation for H57 concerns PTEN loss in the clean TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1-intact background. Discovery was +0.252 [0.169, 0.335], with a lower bound above the 0.15-unit threshold; independent validation was +0.186 [0.066, 0.307]. The validation point estimate remains above the scientific-effect threshold and is directionally consistent, but its lower bound falls below 0.15, so it does not independently satisfy the threshold at the stringent validation level. I therefore retain H57 as an accepted within-dataset association based primarily on the original discovery result and the broader validated PTEN-loss context, while explicitly noting that this specific clean-background effect is supported rather than independently threshold-confirmed by validation. I keep the investigation closed because further redundant validation of the same overall PTEN association would be less informative than moving to untested robustness and context questions. The new validation for H66 tests PTEN loss among KEAP1-loss samples in the stronger-dependency direction. Discovery was +0.378 [0.193, 0.562], with a lower bound above threshold. Independent validation was +0.409 [0.094, 0.724], a slightly larger point estimate but with an interval lower bound below threshold because the exposed cell contains only 49 observations. I retain H66 as accepted: the original evidence is scientifically meaningful in this supplied dataset, and the validation supports the same direction and magnitude point estimate, but the validation is too imprecise to serve as independent threshold confirmation. The context-specific PTEN association is therefore accepted with a meaningful uncertainty caveat and closed for further immediate follow-up. The new validation for H78 concerns sex, male versus female, in the registered negative-signed direction. Discovery gave +0.074 [0.010, 0.138], statistically nonzero in the claimed direction but far below the 0.15-unit threshold, with an upper bound below threshold. Validation gave -0.013 [-0.104, 0.077], now opposite the claimed direction and compatible with zero. I retain the rejection and close H78: sex does not support a scientifically meaningful association at the pre-specified threshold in this supplied dataset. More broadly, the supported core within-dataset associations remain SMARCA4-loss stronger dependency, TP53-loss weaker dependency, KEAP1-loss weaker dependency, and PTEN-loss stronger dependency, with accepted subtype or background contexts for the core predictors. Negative findings remain useful: many alteration, technical, and global predictors did not reach the scientific threshold. The principal remaining uncertainty is now less about overall PTEN association and more about imprecise context modification, especially PTEN by TP53-loss or SMARCA4-loss backgrounds, non-Avana library contexts, adult-category robustness, subtype interactions, and the still-unexamined constructed marker assays. The most informative next work should therefore shift from repeating overall PTEN confirmation to executing the registered robustness and broad discovery tests: adult-category contrasts for SMARCA4, TP53, and KEAP1; subtype-interaction tests for the three core predictors; remaining research-marker tests, and the broader media contrast. PTEN-by-TP53 context tests should be followed only if the analysis stage can improve power or pooling; adding more small subgroup cuts is now less valuable. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations of measured predictors with the composite dependency score, not causal knockout effects or externally generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Round 6 synthesis. H57 clean TP53/SMARCA4/KEAP1-intact PTEN: discovery +0.252 [0.169,0.335], validation +0.186 [0.066,0.307]; keep accepted/closed, context supported but not independently threshold-confirmed. H66 PTEN among KEAP1-loss: discovery +0.378 [0.193,0.562], validation +0.409 [0.094,0.724]; keep accepted/closed, directionally validated but validation imprecise. H78 sex male-female: discovery +0.074 [0.010,0.138], validation -0.013 [-0.104,0.077]; reject/closed. Core supported within-dataset set: SMARCA4 loss stronger dependency; TP53 loss weaker dependency; KEAP1 loss weaker dependency; PTEN loss stronger dependency overall and in accepted contexts. Negative: KRAS, MTAP, ARID1A, STK11, MYC amp, BRCA2, CDKN2A, marker D, EGFR amp, PIK3CA, RB1, ERBB2 amp, APC, SMAD4, NF1, technical/global probes, sex. Unresolved/deferred: PTEN-by-TP53 contexts H79/H80/H71, PTEN-in-SMARCA4-loss H65, non-Avana PTEN H68, KEAP1-by-TP53/high-serum contexts, adult robustness H33/H34/H70, subtype interactions H82-H84, marker E/F H38/H39, media 10 vs 2 H63. Next: prioritize registered adult/subtype/marker/media robustness and broad discovery; avoid redundant overall PTEN validation."
